{
  "name": "goose",
  "version": 4,
  "introduced_by": "glassfall",
  "notes": "paddle: Canada goose, about 0.9 m long and 0.75 m to the top of the head. A boat-shaped body that sits low and level on water, a long black neck held up, a black head with a white chin strap, a flat broad bill, a brown back over paler flanks barred brown, white under a short black tail, black legs set back with webbed feet. Gaggles float near the shore, and lift off together, honking, when you come near. The base of every waterfowl: neck and head are one bone (`head`) drawn in the `neck`, `head` and `beak` colours so a child recolours them (mallard: green; swan: white); the back and flank bars use `wing`, the white strap and undertail `white`. Model drawn on a 66x48x52 grid at 0.025 m (30 voxels to the head), as few large overlapping shapes for decimation: a chest, body and rump ellipsoid, a tapering neck of three segments, a flattened bill, two-voxel eyes in their own `eye` colour (children's neck carve removes them; mallard and swan add their own), thick legs with flattened webbed feet.",
